This Next.js site already has a blog. Add Rankdesk as an extra content source for it, without changing the design and without removing anything that is already there.

Rankdesk content API
Base URL: https://app.rankdesk.ai/api/public/v1
Auth header on every request: "x-api-key: rd_live_6365f585c11e23c42f33b211d881d35b8e294649498f270a"

Endpoints:
- GET /posts?kind=blog&limit=20&offset=0
  -> { "posts": [ { slug, kind, title, excerpt, content, html, youtube_url, meta_title, meta_description, image_url, image_alt, image_width, image_height, primary_keyword, word_count, published_at, updated_at, toc, json_ld } ] }
- GET /posts/{slug}
  -> { "post": { ...same fields } }


Types (copy these exactly into the helper file so TypeScript never has to guess, and never use "any"):
type RankdeskTocItem = { level: number; text: string; id: string };
type RankdeskPost = {
  slug: string; kind: "blog" | "page"; title: string;
  excerpt: string | null; content: string; html: string;
  youtube_url: string | null; meta_title: string | null; meta_description: string | null;
  image_url: string | null; image_alt: string | null;
  image_width: number | null; image_height: number | null;
  image_local_path: string | null; primary_keyword: string | null;
  word_count: number | null; published_at: string | null; updated_at: string | null;
  toc: RankdeskTocItem[]; json_ld: Record<string, unknown>[];
};
type RankdeskListResponse = { posts: RankdeskPost[] };
type RankdeskPostResponse = { post: RankdeskPost };
Every nullable field above really can be null, so guard before use instead of asserting with "!". Do not widen or narrow the site's existing post type to fit this shape: map Rankdesk fields onto it in one small adapter function.

Field notes:
- "html" is the article body already rendered to HTML, including tables and a responsive YouTube iframe. Render this field, not "content". Inject it as raw HTML (for example dangerouslySetInnerHTML) so the video shows as an embedded player.
- "content" is the same body as Markdown, kept only as a fallback. It can contain a "[youtube](url)" line, which is a video embed placeholder, never a text link. If you render Markdown instead of "html", turn that line into an iframe for https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID and never output the word "youtube" as a link.
- If a Markdown renderer such as react-markdown is used anyway, never spread its props onto DOM elements: destructure and drop "node" first, otherwise every paragraph ships an invalid node="[object Object]" attribute. Also drop whitespace-only children in custom table, thead, tbody and tr components so the page source does not fill up with blank lines.
- "youtube_url" is the video URL of that embed, or null.
- "image_url" is an absolute URL for the featured image and may be null. "image_alt" is the alt text for it: always put it in the alt attribute of the featured image, and never ship an <img> without alt text. Body images already carry alt text in "html" and "content", so keep those alt attributes exactly as delivered. Never render an <img> that has an alt attribute but an empty, missing or undefined src: if a localized image is not available yet, fall back to the absolute "image_url" (or the original body image URL) for that one image and adopt it on the next sync, and never strip the src while keeping the tag.
- "image_width" and "image_height" are the pixel size of the featured image, or null. When they are present, set them as width and height attributes so the layout does not jump while the image loads, and add loading="lazy" plus decoding="async" to every image below the fold.
- "meta_title" and "meta_description" are ready for the page head. Fall back to "title" and "excerpt" when they are null.
- "updated_at" is the last change date. Put it in the page as a "Last updated" line and use it for the lastmod value in the sitemap, next to "published_at" for the publish date.
- "toc" is a list of { level, text, id } for the H2 and H3 headings. The ids already exist in "html", so you can render a table of contents with links to "#id" without touching the body.
- "json_ld" is a ready array of JSON-LD objects for this piece. Output it as is inside one <script type="application/ld+json"> tag per object, and do not build your own article schema next to it. Its image URLs already point at this site's own domain under "/images/content/", so never swap them back to a remote URL.
- "image_local_path" is the exact path this site must store the featured image at, for example "/images/content/{slug}-{file name}". Save the downloaded file there and use that path everywhere: the <img> tag, Open Graph, Twitter and the sitemap. It matches the URL inside "json_ld".
- Links inside "content" that point to another domain must open in a new tab with rel="noopener noreferrer". Links to this site's own pages always open in the same tab.
- Only published articles are returned, so never filter again on the client.

Activate it like this (do these steps now, in order):
1. Create a server secret named RANKDESK_API_KEY with the key value above. Never put the key in client code, in a VITE_ variable, or in a committed file.
2. Add one server side helper, for example "src/lib/rankdesk.server.ts", that calls the base URL with the "x-api-key" header, reads the key with process.env inside the function body, disables persistent caching, and returns a typed list and single article.
3. Wire that helper into the blog route loaders or server functions.
4. Verify: load the blog index and one article and confirm the HTML returned by the server already contains the article text.
5. Make the POST /api/rankdesk/sync route public. Exclude it from any site wide middleware, auth guard, session check, login wall, or API key gate. Rankdesk cannot send a session cookie or extra secret with that ping, so a 401 or 403 there stops every update and "Save & Update" will fail.

Hard requirements:
- Fetch server side only, inside route loaders or server functions. Never fetch this API from browser code, and never expose the API key to the browser.
- Do not cache Rankdesk API responses indefinitely. Use cache: "no-store" where supported, or revalidate at most every 60 seconds. A newly published or republished article must appear without rebuilding or redeploying this site.
- Read the key with process.env inside the handler, not at module scope.
- Every article page must set a canonical URL pointing at this site's own domain.
- The sitemap must include every Rankdesk article. If the sitemap is a static file it cannot stay in sync, so replace it with a server generated sitemap route that fetches the article list from this API at request time and adds one <url> entry per article at the path pattern this site uses, with <lastmod> from "updated_at" or "published_at". Keep every URL the sitemap already contains. If a sitemap index is used, add these URLs to the child sitemap that covers them.
- If this site already serves an "/llms.txt", the articles must be listed there too. Only touch it when it already exists, never create one here. Keep its existing structure, H1, summary and sections exactly as they are, keep every link that is already listed, and add the articles under the matching existing section, or under a new section when none exists, one line per item in the form "- [Title](/path/slug): short description from the excerpt or meta description". If the file is static it will go stale, so make it a server generated route that pulls the list from this API at request time, and cap the list at the 50 most recent items.
- Rankdesk pings IndexNow so new and updated URLs get picked up within minutes. IndexNow checks ownership through a key file at the site root, so add a server route that matches "/{key}.txt" where {key} is a 32 character hexadecimal string, and return that same string as plain text with content type "text/plain". It must only match that exact pattern at the root, nothing else.
- Handle a failed request by rendering an empty state and keeping any other content sources working, never a crash. If the sitemap or llms.txt request fails, still output everything else.

Own the content permanently (build this from the start, it is not optional):
- IMPORTANT FOR LOVABLE AND OTHER SERVERLESS HOSTS: the deployed application filesystem is read-only. Never use node:fs, writeFile, mkdir, process.cwd(), repository Markdown files or public/ as runtime storage. That approach can appear to work in preview and then returns 500 "local storage unavailable" after publishing.
- Store every fetched article in the site's own durable backend database. On Lovable, use Lovable Cloud and create a neutral table such as "site_content" keyed by slug and kind. Store title, excerpt, Markdown, localized HTML, meta fields, image path, image metadata, dates, toc and json_ld. Add the required grants and RLS policies. This owned database copy remains after the connection is switched off. Add a source field or equivalent marker so routes can distinguish a Rankdesk-managed row from the site's original content.
- Download every image an item uses, both "image_url" and every image inside the body, into the site's own durable object storage in a neutral public bucket such as "content-images". Use an object key named "{slug}/{original file name}". Store and render only the resulting customer-site URL. Never attempt to write images into public/ at runtime.
- Image adoption applies to <img> sources and the featured "image_url" only. An <iframe> src, such as the YouTube embed inside "html", is NOT an image: never download it, never rewrite it, and never store its response body. Leave every <iframe> and its src exactly as delivered. Treating an embed URL as media replaces the video with the embed page's raw HTML and breaks the page.
- Secure that bucket properly or the site's security scan will flag "uploaded files can be read, modified or deleted by anyone". Make the "content-images" bucket public for reading, and add RLS policies on storage.objects scoped to that bucket: allow public/anon SELECT only for bucket_id = 'content-images', and grant no INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE to anon or authenticated for it. All writes happen server side from the sync route using the service role key, which bypasses RLS, so no write policy is needed. Never leave storage.objects without policies.
- Treat "image_local_path" as the preferred public URL path when the site's storage layer can serve that path. Otherwise replace that path consistently in HTML, Markdown, Open Graph, Twitter and json_ld with the permanent customer-owned storage URL.
- Read routes from the site's database first. Use the API only in the server-side sync/import path to discover and upsert new or changed items. Rendering a public page must never depend on a live API request. For every slug imported from Rankdesk, the owned database row is the authoritative render source and must win over any old bundled Markdown, JSON, import.meta.glob result, static post array, build-time copy or legacy CMS row with the same slug. Existing non-Rankdesk posts keep their original source priority. Remove or stop reading the obsolete static copy after its database migration succeeds.
- Localizing images is part of the single write path, not a one time migration. Every write, including updates and re-syncs of an item that already exists, must run the same download and rewrite step, so a refreshed item can never reintroduce a remote image URL. Never write "html" or "content" to disk or to the page before the rewrite has run, and never render a stored item whose body still contains a remote image URL: download it first.
- Never delete stored files because the API stopped returning an item.
- Storing is not a one time import. Add a server route "POST /api/rankdesk/sync" that runs the exact same database upsert and object-storage image adoption step for any item that is new or updated, and returns JSON like { "synced": 3 }. Rankdesk calls this route within seconds of publishing a new article or page, and also right after a customer edits and saves a published item, so the owned copy stays current. It takes an optional JSON body { "event", "items": [{ "slug", "kind" }] }: use it as a hint when present, and fall back to a full check of the API list when it is empty. It must be safe to call repeatedly, must never delete anything, and must not require the caller to send a secret. It must also be publicly reachable: do not put it behind auth middleware, session guards, login walls, or any API key gate, and do not require a cookie or an Authorization header. Rankdesk cannot send a session or secret with that ping, so a 401 or 403 there breaks every update.
- The sync route is an overwrite route, not an "add missing items" route. This is the part that is most often built wrong. For every slug named in "items" it must call the Rankdesk API for that exact item, adopt its images into customer-owned object storage, rewrite every image reference, and UPSERT the complete database row even when that item already exists and even when timestamps look unchanged. Never skip an item because it is already stored. Never compare a hash first. When "items" is empty, do the same upsert for every item the API returns.
- The JSON it returns must report what it actually rewrote: "synced" is the number of items written to storage in this call, and it must be at least 1 whenever a named slug was found in the API. Also return an "items" array containing the exact persisted revision for every successful item, for example { "synced": 1, "items": [{ "slug": "example", "updated_at": "2026-08-25T08:16:12.290741+00:00" }] }. Read each row back from the database after the upsert and use that read-back value in the response. Rankdesk verifies the requested slug and updated_at exactly, so a stale row, hardcoded success, write that was not awaited, or response without this revision confirmation is a failed update.
- Never let the route swallow errors and answer 200 with zeros. If the API call fails, return the HTTP status and an "error" string so the failure is visible.
- Isolate failures per item and per image: wrap each item's fetch-and-overwrite, and each individual image download inside it, in its own try/catch. A single missing, empty or failing image URL must only skip that one image (keep whatever local copy already exists for it) and must never throw out of the whole request. Collect any per-item failures into an "errors": [{ "slug", "error" }] array in the response instead of letting one bad item crash the batch and turn every other named item into a failed sync too.
- Also run that same sync on a schedule, at least once a day (a cron trigger, a scheduled task, or on the first request of the day), so nothing depends on the ping arriving.
- Until an item is stored in the site's database and its images are adopted into the site's storage, do not render it: run the sync for that item first, then render only the owned copy.
- Images must actually appear on the live page, both the featured image and every image inside the body. The article template must render the stored featured image as a real <img> at the top of the page, and must also use it for Open Graph and Twitter image tags instead of a generic site-wide fallback image. When the stored image path or storage URL is missing for a row, re-run the adoption for that row instead of rendering the article without an image.
- Do not sanitize, filter or rewrite the body HTML in a way that drops <img> tags or their src attributes. Many HTML sanitizers and Markdown renderers strip img, figure, iframe or table elements by default: allow img (with src, alt, width, height, loading, decoding), figure, figcaption, iframe, table, thead, tbody, tr, th and td. If the rendered page shows alt text without a picture, the sanitizer or the image adoption step is the cause, not the API.

- If an earlier version of this integration used "public/images/rankdesk/", move all its files into "public/images/content/", replace every "/images/rankdesk/" reference across the project with "/images/content/", remove the old folder, and rename presentation-only identifiers such as "rankdesk-html" to a neutral name such as "article-content".
- Before finishing, delete any earlier runtime-filesystem sync implementation and all imports that support it. Search all route loaders and server functions for the old static source. For a slug managed by Rankdesk, no route may return that old source before querying the owned database row.
- End-to-end verification is mandatory: change a distinctive sentence in an already-existing stored item upstream, call the deployed POST /api/rankdesk/sync with that slug and its updated_at, require a 200 response with synced >= 1 and an items entry containing the exact same slug and updated_at, then fetch the public article URL with a cache-busting query parameter. Confirm its server-rendered HTML contains the new sentence and no longer contains the previous sentence, and that the featured image and every body image are present with a non-empty customer-owned src that returns HTTP 200. Do not declare completion based only on the sync JSON or database row.
- Verify permanence and source cleanliness: with the API key temporarily unavailable to the public page renderer, the blog index and one article must still render fully from the site's database and every image must load from a customer-owned URL. Inspect the final server-rendered HTML, all JSON-LD scripts, Open Graph/Twitter tags, src and srcset attributes, and confirm there is no "rankdesk" text or URL anywhere in those outputs. Internal server-only integration module names do not affect page source, but no such name may be emitted to HTML, class names or data attributes.

Next.js specifics (App Router):
1. Keep the existing blog routes, layout, components, styling and the current CMS or data source exactly as they are. Do not delete, disable or replace them.
2. Put the key in ".env.local" as RANKDESK_API_KEY and add it in the hosting project settings too. Never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
3. Create "lib/rankdesk.ts" with "import 'server-only'" at the top and fetch the endpoints above with the "x-api-key" header and "cache: 'no-store'".
4. In the blog index route, merge owned Rankdesk database rows with the existing posts, de-duplicated by slug and sorted by publish date descending. For Rankdesk-managed slugs, the owned database row wins over bundled/static data. Original posts that were never imported keep using the existing source.
5. In the article route, load Rankdesk-managed slugs from the owned database first and use the existing source only for slugs not managed by Rankdesk. Never fetch Rankdesk directly while rendering. Map the owned fields onto the existing post shape: title, excerpt, Markdown body, featured image, publish date, meta title and meta description. Do not change the existing types in a way that breaks current posts.
6. Extend generateStaticParams and generateMetadata so Rankdesk articles are prerendered and get canonical, Open Graph and Twitter tags on this site's own domain.
7. If the Rankdesk request fails or the key is missing, log it server side and render the existing posts as normal.
8. Extend "app/sitemap.ts" with the Rankdesk article URLs at the same path pattern the blog uses, keeping every existing entry. If the sitemap is a static file in "public", replace it with "app/sitemap.ts" that keeps the same URLs and adds the articles.
9. If this site serves an "/llms.txt", add the Rankdesk articles to it as well, following the llms.txt rule above, keeping every existing line intact.
10. Permanence in Next.js: serverless filesystems are read-only at runtime, so never use node:fs or write into content/ or public/ from a request. Store every fetched article in a durable database and adopt its images into durable object storage, rewriting every reference before the database upsert. No image URL or public image path in rendered HTML, metadata or structured data may contain "rankdesk". Read the owned database rows first and use the API only from the server-side sync/import path. Never delete an owned row because the API stopped returning it.

Then confirm the blog index shows both sources merged, one Rankdesk article page renders server side in "view source", "/sitemap.xml" contains that article URL, an existing article still works unchanged, and the Rankdesk articles and their images still render with RANKDESK_API_KEY removed.
